condition_name: "Chancroid (Ulcus Molle, Soft Chancre)"
condition_slug: chancroid
source: NSTG 2022
introduction: "An infectious disease caused by Haemophilus ducreyi, a small gram-negative bacillus. Common in the tropics, especially in Africa, the Far East, and the Caribbean. Persons may present with chancroid outside endemic regions; sporadic outbreaks of infection occur in Europe and North America."
clinical_features[1]:
  - type: Clinical Features
    features[18]: Incubation period is about 3 - 7 days,"Begins as a small, tender papule, changing into a pustule which rapidly progresses to a painful ulcer with a bright red areola",Neither the edge nor base of the ulcer is indurated (unlike syphilis),"The ulcer feels soft, hence the name 'soft sore' (ulcus molle)","With superimposed bacterial infection, it often feels indurated",The ulcers may be multiple due to auto-inoculation,"Sites of predilection in men are the prepuce, frenulum, glans or shaft of the penis","In women the labia, fourchette, vestibule, clitoris, cervix, or perineum are favoured sites","Lesions may cause dyspareunia, pain on voiding or defaecation and vaginal discharge",Women may be asymptomatic carriers,"About 7 - 14 days after the appearance of the ulcer, a bubo appears","A mass of glands matted together, often adherent to the overlying skin","The glands above the inguinal ligament are usually affected, and often there is a unilateral enlargement",Central softening is often found and if untreated the bubo may rupture and discharge through a fistula,The combination of a painful genital ulcer and suppurative inguinal adenopathy is almost pathognomonic of chancroid,"Patient may present with bubo, the initial ulcer having healed",Atypical lesions have been reported in HIV-infected individuals,"More extensive, or multiple lesions sometimes accompanied by systemic manifestations such as fever and chills"
investigations[2]: "Microscopy, culture and sensitivity of discharge from ulcer",Serological tests e.g. complement fixation (CF); microimmuno-fluorescence (MIF) test; PCR
treatment:
  goals[1]: Same as for Gonorrhoea
  non_drug[2]: Keep ulcerative lesions clean,"Aspirate fluctuant lymph nodes through the surrounding healthy skin, preferably from a superior approach to prevent persistent dripping and sinus formation"
  drug[4]: Ciprofloxacin 500 mg orally every 12 hours for 3 days,Erythromycin 500 mg orally every 6 hours for 7 days,Azithromycin 1 g orally as a single dose,"Ceftriaxone, 250 mg by intramuscular injection, as a single dose"
  adverse_reactions_and_cautions[2]: Ciprofloxacin and ceftriaxone (see gonorrhoea),Erythromycin and azithromycin (see chlamydia)
  supportive_measures[3]: Follow-up until there is clear evidence of improvement or cure,"In HIV-infected patients, treatment may appear to be less effective. Follow-up weekly until there is clear evidence of improvement",Chancroid and HIV infection are closely associated and therapeutic failure is likely to be seen with increasing frequency
differential_diagnoses[10]: Syphilis,Herpes,Granuloma inguinale,Lymphogranuloma venereum,Fixed drug eruption,Erythema multiforme,Behcet's disease,Trauma,Tuberculous ulcer,Cancers
complications[1]: "Progressive ulceration and amputation of the phallus, particularly in HIV patients"
prevention[4]: Counselling,Compliance,Condom use,Contact treatment
other_investigations: null
definitive_treatment: null
prognosis: null